  "narrative": {
    "intro": "New York received $7.4B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The federal contracting market in New York recorded 227,266 awards so far, with an average award size of $32.7K. Obligations fell 38.5% year over year. Fiscal-year obligations trended down from $30.8B in FY21, to $29.4B in FY22, to $16.5B in FY23, to $15.1B in FY24, to $12.1B in FY25, and to $7.4B in FY26. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $3.9B. Energy and veterans programs also show substantial activity, with the Department of Energy obligating $819M and the Department of Veterans Affairs obligating $660M. BROOKHAVEN SCIENCE ASSOCIATES LLC is the top vendor at $830M, and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Biotechnology) is the top industry at $830M. All figures are contract obligations; current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed year over year in New York federal contracting?",
        "body": "Year-over-year obligations fell 38.5%. Fiscal-year obligations declined from $30.8B in FY21 to $7.4B in FY26. The decline shows up in total obligations and in the aggregated pace of awards to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors dominate New York federal spending?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $3.9B and is the largest buying agency. The Department of Energy obligated $819M and the Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $660M. BROOKHAVEN SCIENCE ASSOCIATES LLC obligated $830M, and LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION appears multiple times among top vendors with $496M, $307M, and $122M entries."
      }
    ]
  },
